EVERY HALFWORD THAT FORMS AN ADDRESS FROM THE EXECUTION POSITION OR STACK POINTER.

Status: English Major C experimental ARMv7-A T32 normative draft.
Finite closed decoding region: ALL and ONLY halfwords 0xA000 through 0xAFFF.
This is a complete 4096-halfword CLASSIFICATION of two 16-bit instruction forms,
not a complete ARM ISA, compiler, or processor implementation.
The source-level permission grant remains separate.

PRIMARY ARCHITECTURE:
Arm Limited, ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A and ARMv7-R edition,
ARM DDI 0406C.d, ID040418. Consult A6 16-bit Thumb instruction decoding
and A8's ADR and ADD (SP plus immediate) descriptions.
Secondary cross-check: the A-profile A32/T32 ADR and ADD (SP plus immediate)
instruction descriptions; the secondary reference may contain variants
from another architectural revision and does not expand the declared grant.

The entire 4096-row source enumeration is in the repository file
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-address-generation-halfwords.tsv.
No table cell is itself an executable native instruction.
All values in that table describe architecture bitfields and arithmetic.
The table's example numeric register states MUST NOT be confused
with actual loaded Android process addresses.

PART ONE. PRECISE INVENTORY OF THE MACHINE OBJECTS USED.

The current instruction begins at a virtual BYTE address called A.
This is the address of the FIRST halfword of the instruction.
It is NOT an ordinal instruction number. In the present T32
instruction state, the current instruction must have a
halfword-aligned starting byte address.

The abstract EXECUTION INDEX is the next native instruction
address selected by architectural sequencing and legal
control transfers. These source instructions do not branch.
Their normal next execution index is A+2, because each
encoding in this finite region is exactly TWO BYTES long.

The machine has a program-counter register identified
as R15. When these T32 instructions use the PC as an
ordinary PC-relative arithmetic source, they use the
instruction address A plus four bytes. The ADR operation
also explicitly discards the low two bits of that
PC-operand value, obtaining Align(A+4,4).
This produces a multiple of FOUR bytes even when A+4
itself is congruent to two modulo four.

The machine has an SP register, R13, whose current
32-bit bit pattern may be used in numerical address
arithmetic. Reading SP as a source does NOT alter it.
Adding an immediate to SP and putting the result into
a DIFFERENT destination is not an instruction to
grow/shrink the process stack.

The machine has eight named low general-purpose
registers R0 through R7 that can be selected
by a three-bit field in either of the two
instruction forms in this region.
Neither SP, LR, PC, nor registers R8–R12 can be
the encoded destination of these sixteen-bit forms.
Their actual 32-bit values are arbitrary inputs
except where the operation writes a destination.

The machine has four relevant comparison flags:
negative, zero, carry and signed-overflow.
Neither instruction here recalculates any of
those flags merely by forming an address.

The physical memory map, caches, DRAM, instruction
prefetch mechanism and permissions are distinct
from architectural REGISTER ADDRESS ARITHMETIC.
Neither operation in this region performs a
data-memory load, data-memory store or
an architecture-defined branch.

PART TWO. DECODE ALL 4096 POSSIBLE HALFWORDS.

Every halfword from 0xA000 to 0xAFFF has
four high bits equal to binary 1010.
The fifth bit distinguishes the two
exactly allocated instruction families.

If the highest five bits are 10100,
the halfword is in 0xA000–0xA7FF
and selects the Thumb T1 ADR family,
a PC-relative address computation.

If the highest five bits are 10101,
the halfword is in 0xA800–0xAFFF
and selects the Thumb T1 ADD (SP plus immediate)
family, writing a result to a low register.

The following THREE bits select a destination
general-purpose register numbered zero
through seven. The lowest EIGHT bits form
one unsigned eight-bit immediate value,
called imm8, which ranges from zero
through 255 inclusive.

For each instruction family, the destination
has eight choices and the immediate has
256 choices. Eight times 256 gives
exactly 2048 distinct allocated halfwords.
Two instruction families therefore give
4096 distinct values, covering every
hexadecimal value A000–AFFF without overlap.

For both instruction families, the actual
BYTE displacement is exactly FOUR times
the unsigned encoded imm8 value. The
largest permitted displacement is 4*255,
or 1020 bytes. Negative displacement
is not representable by these two native
sixteen-bit instruction forms.

The destination is the three-bit value
((halfword >> 8) AND binary 111).
The immediate is (halfword AND hexadecimal FF).
Neither bitfield contains a branch condition,
branch target register, code-versus-data tag
or privilege bit.

PART THREE. COMPUTE THE PC-RELATIVE ADDRESS WITHOUT FETCHING DATA.

For a halfword A000–A7FF, read the starting byte
address A, calculate the architectural T32
PC-operand value A+4, then round that value
DOWN to the nearest integer divisible by four.
Call this aligned integer BASE.

Read the encoded unsigned imm8.
Calculate DISPLACEMENT=4*imm8.
Calculate RESULT=(BASE+DISPLACEMENT) modulo 2^32.
Write RESULT to the encoded destination R0–R7.

Do NOT load the data contents of RESULT.
Do NOT fetch a native instruction from RESULT.
Do NOT branch to RESULT.
Do NOT overwrite SP, LR, or the flags N/Z/C/V.
Do NOT change the memory at RESULT.
The normal sequential execution index becomes A+2.

In ARM assembler nomenclature this form is
ADR T1. Its purpose is to FORM an address as
an ordinary 32-bit register value.
The number produced might happen to be:
  the byte address of data in a literal pool;
  a byte address in executable code;
  the byte address of a string;
  a numerically valid but currently unmapped address;
  an address that has no useful intended referent.

The mathematical result does not itself
establish which of those meanings, if any,
the application intended. Permissions and
subsequent operations determine effects.

PART FOUR. THE EXECUTION INDEX, PC OPERAND AND ALIGNED BASE ARE DIFFERENT.

At instruction address A=0x00001000:
  next sequential execution index: 0x00001002;
  normal PC-operand reading: 0x00001004;
  Align(PC,4) for ADR: 0x00001004.

At instruction address A=0x00001002:
  next sequential execution index: 0x00001004;
  normal PC-operand reading: 0x00001006;
  Align(PC,4) for ADR: 0x00001004.

The two different current instruction addresses
have DIFFERENT next sequential instructions
and DIFFERENT raw PC-operand values, but their
aligned PC bases are IDENTICAL.

For either A=0x1000 or A=0x1002,
halfword A001 encodes ADR R0 with imm8=1,
so the register receives 0x1004+4=0x1008.
The value 0x1008 is not the next executed
instruction and not the value of the
execution index merely by being in R0.

At A=0x1002, halfword A000 places 0x1004
in R0 even though the ordinary next
instruction is at 0x1004 as well.
That accidental numerical equality does
not make ADR a branch: execution advances
by its instruction length and the
register update is still observable.

PART FIVE. COMPUTE AN SP-RELATIVE ADDRESS WITHOUT CHANGING SP.

For a halfword A800–AFFF, take the exact
old architectural SP value. Read imm8,
and compute DISPLACEMENT=4*imm8.
Calculate RESULT=(old_SP+DISPLACEMENT) mod 2^32.
Write RESULT to the encoded destination R0–R7.

SP remains equal to old_SP.
The original selected destination value
is overwritten, but every other general-purpose
register is preserved. LR and PC operand state
are not changed. N/Z/C/V are preserved.
No data memory is read or written.
The normal next execution index is A+2.

This is ADD (SP plus immediate) T1. It
is NOT the previously granted ADD SP,SP,#imm7
T2 instruction which actually changes SP.
The distinction is visible in the DESTINATION.

Example, given old SP=0x00002000:
  A800 => ADD R0, SP, #0; result R0=0x2000.
  A801 => ADD R0, SP, #4; result R0=0x2004.
  A9FF => ADD R1, SP, #1020; result R1=0x23FC.
  AFFF => ADD R7, SP, #1020; result R7=0x23FC.
None of these changes SP=0x2000.
If a later load uses the computed register
as an address, that load is a SEPARATE instruction
with its own access permissions, possible
exceptions, and memory effects.

PART SIX. CONDITIONAL EXECUTION DOES NOT CHANGE THE DECODED BITFIELDS.

When one of these instructions is in a
LEGAL T32 IT block, the established ITSTATE
may suppress its destination write when
the appropriate condition is false.
The native two-byte width remains TWO
even when its ordinary data effect is skipped.
The halfword's destination and immediate
are not re-encoded or replaced.
The next execution index still advances
two bytes for ordinary sequential passage.
No arithmetic condition flags are changed.

An IT block has its own strict limits
and contains whole 16/32-bit instructions.
One cannot assume any arbitrary malformed
surrounding ITSTATE is valid merely because
A001 is a legal ADR encoding in isolation.

PART SEVEN. WHY THIS IS NOT THE SAME AS LDR LITERAL.

An LDR literal instruction also calculates
an address using a PC-relative aligned base.
But LDR then actually READS one word of
DATA from that calculated virtual address.
That can generate a data-memory fault
and requires readable memory permissions.

ADR merely writes the calculated address.
It need not read memory at that address,
and the numerical result may even identify
a presently unmapped address without the
operation itself attempting to fetch it.

The complete T32 T1 LDR literal decoding
region 0x4800–0x4FFF is documented separately
in "Every PC-relative data-word load halfword.english".
The two mechanisms must not be treated as
one source operation under a vague phrase
such as "retrieve the address".

Nor is ADR a branch. A branch uses a computed
or immediate TARGET to replace the execution
index. ADR leaves the execution index on
the ordinary sequential path while writing
a general-purpose register.

PART EIGHT. DESIGN ALTERNATIVES AND WHY THEY WERE CONSIDERED.

Alternative A: use ADR T1 to form a nearby PC-relative
address in one two-byte native instruction.
Advantage: precise compact addressing without
fetching data. Limit: low destination registers,
positive four-byte-scaled offsets, and aligned
PC base. This is granted as operation 40.

Alternative B: use a wider ADR T32 encoding
capable of a larger positive or negative
displacement. Advantage: greater reach.
Cost: four-byte native form, different immediate
fields and possibly a different source target.
It is not implicitly admitted by operation 40.

Alternative C: use ADD (SP plus immediate) T1
to produce an address relative to a known
stack-pointer value without modifying the stack.
Advantage: explicit source register semantics,
compact low-register form.
Cost: no automatic bounds checking and
no memory read. This is operation 73.

Alternative D: change SP with ADD SP,SP,#imm
and then copy it to another register.
This can produce similar numerical outputs
in some cases, but changes observable SP,
may violate ABI stack rules and differs
from operation 73. It is not a valid
single-instruction translation of A800.

Alternative E: load an ADDRESS value from
literal memory using LDR. Advantage: addresses
not directly constructible by a narrow ADR
may be held in literal data. Cost: actual
data-memory read, possible fault, literal
pool storage and distinct encoding.

Alternative F: create a register value with
MOVW and MOVT. Advantage: can build a
chosen absolute integer value without
reading memory. Cost: multiple actual native
instructions and relocation/position
questions. That is not an ADR in source
unless the actual emitted bytes are translated
and shown to have equivalent behavior
under the declared execution environment.

Alternative G: branch to an address instead
of merely forming it. This has a DIFFERENT
architectural effect: it replaces the
execution index and can skip surrounding
instructions. It is not an acceptable
reinterpretation of an observed ADR.

Alternative H: treat the computed address
as a capability-bearing pointer, with
permissions, accessible length and lifetime.
Possible advantage: higher-level checking
and safer interfaces. But the actual
ARM register contains a 32-bit bit pattern;
the typed pointer would be a language-level
structure with explicitly defined semantics
and lowering, not a hardware property
invented by a long variable name.

Alternative I: treat code and data bytes
as interchangeable merely because PC-relative
arithmetic can point to either.
Rejected: the computation of an address
does not by itself establish an allowed
memory access or an executable branch.

All alternatives remain recorded. A later
compiler may optimize when equivalence is
established, but cannot change source-machine
provenance retroactively.

PART NINE. EVIDENCE AND NEGATIVE TESTS.

A foreign clang 17 assembler for
armv7a-none-eabi in Thumb state assembled
all 4096 halfwords as literal .hword
data in a temporary machine-code section.
LLVM objdump 17 independently displayed:
  2048 ADR T1 records;
  2048 ADD (SP plus immediate) T1 records.

A separate verifier checked each recorded
halfword, original code-section byte offset,
encoded destination R0..R7 and unsigned
scaled offset 0..1020.
No mismatch was observed in these 4096
classification/encoding checks.
The compiled data were DISASSEMBLED, not
executed by a processor; the diagnostic
was not a maintained ICK/NDK build.

A future independent accepted validator MUST reject:
  (1) treating A001 at A=0x1002 as PC+4+4=0x100A
      without aligning the PC operand first;
  (2) treating ADR as a data memory READ;
  (3) treating ADR as an instruction branch;
  (4) advancing the index by four for a two-byte ADR;
  (5) treating imm8 as an unscaled number of bytes;
  (6) treating the unsigned imm8 as signed;
  (7) permitting R8..R12, SP, LR or PC as the
      destination of either T1 encoding;
  (8) modifying SP when A801 writes R0;
  (9) claiming a stack word was stored by A801;
  (10) changing N/Z/C/V without another instruction;
  (11) asserting a computed address is mapped or
      executable merely because it fits 32 bits;
  (12) calling all of T32 complete after this region;
  (13) claiming that a disassembly is proof of
      native ARM execution or a working language
      implementation.

The finite data-row classification has been
diagnostically checked. A formal or executable
English Major C semantics, invalid-case tests
and actual target acceptance remain outstanding.

END OF A000..AFFF ENCODING CONTRACT.
